└── Modbus TCP client for industrial field I/O (read/write registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : Different response object models: pymodbus returns objects with isError()/registers, pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk often return raw lists or 
    │   None on failure, requiring error-handling rewrites; Connection and unit/slave-id parameter naming differs across libraries (e.g., 'unit' vs 'slave'); 
    │   modbus-tk uses a different API structure (master.execute with function codes) requiring more substantial glue code; minimalmodbus is serial-only and 
    │   not a viable TCP replacement without a hardware-level protocol converter
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── pymodbus is used here as a full-featured Modbus TCP client with a connect/read/write register API and response objects exposing isError()/registers. 
        pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk are TCP-capable Modbus client libraries that provide comparable connect/read/write register semantics, making them plausible 
        substitutes with a compatibility shim. minimalmodbus is excluded since it targets serial/RTU communication with a fundamentally different API (no TCP 
        socket client, simpler read/write float/register calls, different error handling via exceptions rather than response objects), making it a poor fit 
        despite being suggested.

